-- nanoforms, where the regulation states the construction's own hypothesis as a filing requirement.
--
-- REACH's amended Annex VI (Regulation 2018/1881, in force from 1 January 2020) defines a nanoform as a
-- form of a substance containing particles, unbound or as an aggregate or agglomerate, where for 50% or
-- more of the particles in the number size distribution one or more external dimensions lies in the range
-- 1 to 100 nm -- with fullerenes, graphene flakes and single-wall carbon nanotubes below 1 nm included by
-- derogation. Each nanoform is characterised by 2.4.1 its name or identifier, 2.4.2 the number-based
-- particle size distribution with the number fraction in that range, 2.4.3 the description of surface
-- functionalisation or treatment with each agent identified by IUPAC name and CAS or EC number, 2.4.4
-- shape, aspect ratio and other morphological characterisation including crystallinity and assembly
-- structure, and 2.4.5 the specific surface area. So a nanoform's identity is a tuple of measured
-- quantities, which is what this construction says an identity is.
--
-- a nanoform: the molecule, the number fraction in the nanoscale range, the shape, the surface treatment,
-- the specific surface area and the crystal phase, each as the number a measurement returned
Nanoform : Type 1
Nanoform = (M : Mol) × ((frac : Nat) × ((shape : Nat) × ((surface : Nat) × ((ssa : Nat) × Nat))))
